WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.698
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.998 --> 00:00:09.338
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.638 --> 00:00:17.271
Unit twenty-nine: Memory and History. In this unit, you will discuss how societies remember the past.

4
00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:19.598
collective memory.

5
00:00:20.298 --> 00:00:24.318
collective memory means the shared memories of a group or nation.

6
00:00:24.618 --> 00:00:27.755
War leaves deep marks on a nation's collective memory.

7
00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:31.873
Your turn. Say: collective memory.

8
00:00:34.573 --> 00:00:35.760
collective memory.

9
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.274
historical narrative.

10
00:00:38.974 --> 00:00:43.667
historical narrative means the story a society tells about its past.

11
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:46.907
Every country builds its own historical narrative.

12
00:00:47.607 --> 00:00:51.733
Your turn. Say: historical narrative.

13
00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:55.945
historical narrative.

14
00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.148
commemoration.

15
00:00:58.848 --> 00:01:03.182
commemoration means an event or act that honours and remembers the past.

16
00:01:03.482 --> 00:01:05.853
The commemoration was held in silence.

17
00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:09.820
Your turn. Say: commemoration.

18
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:13.649
commemoration.

19
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:15.848
revisionism.

20
00:01:16.548 --> 00:01:22.240
revisionism means reinterpreting history, sometimes to deny or distort facts.

21
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.956
Historians warn against revisionism that denies suffering.

22
00:01:26.656 --> 00:01:29.471
Your turn. Say: revisionism.

23
00:01:32.171 --> 00:01:33.137
revisionism.

24
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.465
oral testimony.

25
00:01:36.165 --> 00:01:40.091
oral testimony means spoken accounts from people who lived through events.

26
00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:43.494
Oral testimony from survivors preserves history.

27
00:01:44.194 --> 00:01:47.369
Your turn. Say: oral testimony.

28
00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:51.360
oral testimony.

29
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.769
reconciliation.

30
00:01:54.469 --> 00:01:59.034
reconciliation means restoring peaceful relationships after conflict.

31
00:01:59.334 --> 00:02:01.903
Reconciliation can take generations.

32
00:02:02.603 --> 00:02:06.288
Your turn. Say: reconciliation.

33
00:02:08.988 --> 00:02:10.245
reconciliation.

34
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.797
transitional justice.

35
00:02:13.497 --> 00:02:20.175
transitional justice means legal and social processes to deal with past abuses after conflict.

36
00:02:20.475 --> 00:02:24.297
Cambodia's tribunal was a form of transitional justice.

37
00:02:24.997 --> 00:02:28.950
Your turn. Say: transitional justice.

38
00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.208
transitional justice.

39
00:02:34.217 --> 00:02:35.427
memorial site.

40
00:02:36.127 --> 00:02:40.437
memorial site means a place created to remember victims of the past.

41
00:02:40.737 --> 00:02:44.640
Memorial sites help younger generations understand history.

42
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.527
Your turn. Say: memorial site.

43
00:02:51.227 --> 00:02:52.483
memorial site.

44
00:02:53.483 --> 00:02:55.773
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:56.073 --> 00:03:00.766
Rithy, how do young Cambodians learn about the Khmer Rouge period?

46
00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:07.509
Mostly from family stories, and arguably more from oral testimony than textbooks.

47
00:03:08.817 --> 00:03:13.257
That's very powerful. Memorial sites play a role too, I suppose.

48
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:19.257
Yes. Visiting them tends to leave a lasting impression on students.

49
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.922
What about the tribunal? Was it seen as successful?

50
00:03:25.217 --> 00:03:31.062
Opinions differ. It could be argued that it brought some justice, though many felt it came late.

51
00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:35.406
Transitional justice is rarely perfect anywhere.

52
00:03:36.717 --> 00:03:42.118
Perhaps reconciliation depends less on courts and more on honest remembrance.

53
00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:46.995
Grammar focus: Hedging in sensitive topics.

54
00:03:47.295 --> 00:03:52.093
Hedging is especially important when discussing sensitive history.

55
00:03:52.393 --> 00:03:59.318
Arguably, family stories teach more than textbooks. Visiting memorials tends to leave a lasting impression.

56
00:03:59.618 --> 00:04:02.964
Opinions differ, and careful language shows respect.

57
00:04:03.264 --> 00:04:10.337
On painful historical topics, hedging shows respect for different experiences and avoids over-generalising.

58
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.176
Now read and listen. Remembering to Heal.

59
00:04:14.476 --> 00:04:20.168
Every society must decide how to remember painful periods of its history.

60
00:04:21.483 --> 00:04:30.402
In Cambodia, the years from 1975 to 1979 remain central to the nation's collective memory.

61
00:04:31.717 --> 00:04:38.627
Memorial sites such as Tuol Sleng and Choeung Ek now help visitors understand what happened.

62
00:04:39.917 --> 00:04:47.652
Between 2006 and 2022, a special tribunal tried senior leaders of the regime.

63
00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:55.106
This process of transitional justice was welcomed by many, although some felt it was too limited.

64
00:04:56.417 --> 00:05:02.108
Oral testimony from survivors has also been recorded to preserve personal histories.

65
00:05:03.417 --> 00:05:07.947
Historians warn that revisionism can arise when such memories are not shared openly.

66
00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:14.907
Commemoration, it seems, is not only about the past but also about building a peaceful future.

67
00:05:16.217 --> 00:05:18.811
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:19.111 --> 00:05:23.200
Restoring peaceful relationships after conflict is blank.

69
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:29.685
A, revisionism. B, reconciliation. C, commemoration.

70
00:05:33.683 --> 00:05:36.786
The answer is B: reconciliation.

71
00:05:38.283 --> 00:05:39.784
Which sentence is hedged?

72
00:05:40.084 --> 00:05:48.311
A, The tribunal failed.. B, It could be argued that the tribunal was too limited.. C, The tribunal was perfect..

73
00:05:52.317 --> 00:05:56.719
The answer is B: It could be argued that the tribunal was too limited..

74
00:05:58.217 --> 00:06:01.261
Young people blank learn history from their families.

75
00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:05.215
A, tend to. B, are tending. C, tends.

76
00:06:09.217 --> 00:06:11.797
The answer is A: tend to.

77
00:06:13.283 --> 00:06:16.572
Spoken accounts from people who lived through events are blank.

78
00:06:16.872 --> 00:06:22.963
A, oral testimony. B, historical narrative. C, memorial sites.

79
00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.925
The answer is A: oral testimony.

80
00:06:31.417 --> 00:06:35.343
According to the text, when did the special tribunal operate?

81
00:06:35.643 --> 00:06:48.596
A, 1975–1979. B, 2006–2022. C, 1993–2000.

82
00:06:52.583 --> 00:06:56.603
The answer is B: 2006–2022.

83
00:06:58.117 --> 00:07:05.018
Now it's your turn. Part one: Why is it important for societies to remember difficult periods of history?

84
00:07:10.318 --> 00:07:16.137
Part two: Use hedging: give two reasons and describe one way of remembering.

85
00:07:22.437 --> 00:07:36.145
It could be argued that remembering protects future generations from repeating mistakes. It also tends to help survivors feel recognised. Recording oral testimony from grandparents is, arguably, one of the most meaningful ways to remember.

86
00:07:36.445 --> 00:07:38.503
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:39.517 --> 00:07:44.630
Great work today! You learned eight new words and hedging in sensitive topics.

88
00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:48.465
Today's value is empathy. Think about how others feel.

89
00:07:48.765 --> 00:07:51.615
See you in Unit thirty. Goodbye!

90
00:07:51.915 --> 00:07:54.602
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

